Gordon,

Confirming Copperline is ready to move on the municipal HVAC CFD engagement (your project number CFDME-2025-033) under our executed MSA (CPL-MSA-2025-014, effective 3 June 2025). Before the next task order goes out I want to restate, in writing, the conditions we are engaging under. None of this is new relative to the signed agreement; I would rather have it on the record than assumed.

1. Place of performance. All analysis, meshing, solves, post-processing, review, and storage or transmission of Copperline or municipal project data must be performed within the United States, by U.S.-located personnel, per MSA Section 7.4. Given the municipal source of funds and the Arizona Uniform Terms and Conditions we operate under, this is a hard requirement, not a preference. Section 7.4 flows down to subcontractors and individual contractors at every tier, and no consent is deemed given by silence or by course of dealing.

2. Subcontracting. If at any point The CFD ME needs to route work — any portion of it — to a party outside the United States, that request must be raised in writing with Copperline BEFORE routing, not after. Section 7.4.3 sets out what the request needs to include (proposed personnel, work location, category of work) and requires enough lead time for us and, where applicable, the municipal customer to evaluate it. A consent granted on one task order does not carry over to another.

3. Rates and this task order's authorization. Hourly billing on this engagement will run against the rate schedule in MSA Section 11, firm through the first twelve months per Section 4.7. I will have TO-004 (Run Batch 4) issued for countersignature later this week — target of 14 August — as a not-to-exceed authorization under the MSA. TO-004 will incorporate Sections 7.4 and 11 by reference without restating them, consistent with Section 2.2.

